/*!
\page qtbridges-csharp-project-templates.html
\title Using project and item templates
\brief Official `dotnet new` templates to build Qt/QML apps with C# using \b{Qt.Bridge.DotNet}.

\section1 What is included

\list
\li \b{Project template} - \c{Qt.Bridge.DotNet Project}

    Creates a minimal C# app wired to Qt Quick / QML. The template includes an
    entry QML (\c{Main.qml}) file and a bootstrap \c{Program.cs} file.

\li  \b{Item template} - \c{Qt.Bridge.DotNet QML File (.qml)}

    Adds a new \c{.qml} file and lets the build integrate it automatically.
\endlist

\section1 How to use

\section2 Install the templates

You can install the templates from local or online NuGet feed:

\list
\li From \b{NuGet feed}:

    \badcode
    dotnet new install __PACKAGE_ID__
    \endcode

\li From a \b{local .nupkg}:

    \badcode
    dotnet new install ./PATH/TO/__PACKAGE_ID__.__PACKAGE_VERSION__.nupkg
    \endcode
\endlist

To verify installation, run:

\badcode
dotnet new list
\endcode

The new templates should appear in the list.

To update the templates to the latest version, run:

\badcode
dotnet new update
# or force a specific package/version
dotnet new install __PACKAGE_ID__ --force
\endcode

\section2 Create a project

To create a new Qt Quick C# project, run:

\badcode
dotnet new qtbridgedotnetproj -n MyQtApp
cd MyQtApp
dotnet build
dotnet run
\endcode

This generates:

\badcode
MyQtApp/
  Project.csproj
  Program.cs
  Main.qml
\endcode

\section2 Add a QML item to an existing project

To add a new QML file to an existing project, in command line, change directory
to the project folder and run:

\badcode
dotnet new qtbridgedotnetqml --FileName=MainPage
\endcode

This creates \c{MainPage.qml}. The build integrates QML files automatically
(they'll be registered and copied alongside your app).

\section2 Uninstall the templates

To uninstall the templates, run:
\badcode
dotnet new uninstall __PACKAGE_ID__
\endcode

*/
